  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1224 PM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nNext Seven Days: Drier and Back to Above Normal Temps Starting Sunday\n\n17z WV satellite shows an upper trough advancing southeastward over \nIA/MN/WI/Lake Superior. On the visible satellite, widespread \nstratocumulus is present across the Upper Midwest. Northerly winds \nare present across the forecast area as a cold front departs to the \nsouth. Temperatures are around 50, just a hair below normal for \nearly November.\n\nWith heights aloft still relatively low as the upper trough slowly \ndeparts, highs in the 40s are expected for Friday. With heights \naloft building through Monday, highs will return to solidly above \nnormal, likely (60-90% along, south of I-94 per 05.13z NBM) in \nthe 60s. The arrival of a pair of upper troughs, the first \nMonday night and the second Wednesday, will moderate \ntemperatures somewhat, with highs in the 50s favored next \nTuesday and Wednesday, still a bit above normal.\n\nAs for precipitation, available moisture with each of the \naforementioned waves is lacking, particularly with Monday night's \ntrough. However, 05.12z GFS does suggest some mid-level saturation \nfollowing the frontal passage associated with the wave next \nWednesday, which could lead to a light post-frontal shower or two if \nthis feature moves through during the daytime hours. Post-frontal \nshowers are a low possiblity for now (15%), mainly appearing in the \noperational GFS and a few 05.00z ECENS members. Either way, impacts \nare not expected.",
